import Mathlib
import SpherePacking.Tactic.EndpointIntegrability.Tactic

/-!
# Integrability corpus (independent check)

Purpose: an independent, unbiased sample of real integrability goals taken from other Lean 4
projects, to be used as a check for integrability automation. Each `example` restates one goal
from a source project as faithfully as possible (same domain, same measure, same integrand
shape; not simplified or normalised). Proofs are intentionally omitted (`sorry`): this file only
records the goals. It is deliberately outside the Lake library so that it can `import Mathlib`;
check it with `lake env lean test/IntegrabilityCorpus.lean` (only `declaration uses 'sorry'`
and unused-variable warnings are expected).

## Status against `endpoint_integrability`

The corpus was collected without reference to any tactic, and the first version of
`endpoint_integrability` was written without access to it. It has been development data since:
after each run, the kinds of goals left open (counted by category, such as "contains a logarithm"
or "inverse of a sum") were used to choose features of the tactic. No rule was written for a
particular entry. The held-out check is the second sample, `test/IntegrabilityCorpus2.lean`.

Each entry carries a `status:` line, written by the scorer (last run on 2026-11-26, with the
tactic at commit 877d268, after the tactic was simplified and features which a `have` replaces
were removed; it stood at 21 before):

* `proved by endpoint_integrability` (19 entries; plain `fun_prop` proves 5): the `sorry` is
  replaced by the tactic call.
* `out of scope: …` (48 entries), assigned only to goals which are not proved, for a structural
  reason read off the tags of the entry: the goal is not an integral over an interval of `ℝ` (22),
  the integrand is an abstract function, so that the goal needs closure rules such as those of
  `fun_prop` and not a solver for explicit integrands (16), or the goal is a domination hypothesis
  and not an integrability goal (10).
* `open: …` (33 entries): not proved and not excluded by its tags, with the goals the tactic left
  or its error.

The `sorry`s of the last two kinds are kept so that the file records the goals. The statuses are
regenerated by `test/scripts/run.sh test/IntegrabilityCorpus.lean <work dir>
endpoint_integrability SpherePacking.Tactic.EndpointIntegrability.Tactic mark`.

## Sources and licence

All source projects are Apache-2.0 licensed; the statements below are taken (restated) from them
with attribution. Every entry names its repository, commit and `file:line`.

* AlexKontorovich/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd @ a4fb6989b07e9c76b511807ae93e33e25b820e9b
* AlexKontorovich/quasi-rh              @ d26d846d6f3129017f06b6fd3f78dd72d361d60b
* PhysLean (HEPLean/PhysLean)            @ 44adced7fc6cbf4e9da05f62fc3143b4d3cd97fc
* TauCetiProject/TauCeti                 @ fb3fbc9f05780f456267a29168dfae7a98c2d8d0
* fpvandoorn/carleson                    @ f7487cc9da4d1e73f009dc0280cd5b958f3e2c78
* RemyDegenne/brownian-motion            @ 8fd8dc52830cdd07cf9cc8eadd533e284425fa6f

## Sampling

Candidates: a regex survey of each project for top-level `theorem`/`lemma` statements whose
conclusion, and in-proof `have`s whose type, mentions `Integrable`, `IntegrableOn`,
`IntervalIntegrable` or `HasFiniteIntegral` (hits on `LocallyIntegrable*`, `IntegrableAtFilter`,
`MellinConvergent` only were filtered out). Domination candidates: call sites of the
dominated-convergence / parametric-integral lemmas (`tendsto_integral_(filter_)of_dominated_convergence`,
`hasSum_integral_of_dominated_convergence`, `hasDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_loc_of_deriv_le`,
`hasFDerivAt_integral_of_dominated_*`, `continuous(At|On|WithinAt)_of_dominated`, ...).
Per project (carleson and brownian-motion pooled), the candidates were sorted by
(project, file, line) and shuffled with Python `random.Random(20261123).shuffle`
(seed 20261123; records and domination sites shuffled by the same generator, records first);
the shuffled list was walked in order until the quota was met. (When the second sample was
drawn, this procedure applied to the saved candidate lists reproduced the order of this sample
only for the PhysLean records and for the PrimeNumberTheoremAnd and PhysLean domination sites;
for the other groups this sample was not drawn in the documented order, for a reason which was
not found. Each entry still cites its source location, but the sample should not be described
as a reproducible random draw.) A drawn record was skipped only for:
(a) it cannot be made self-contained without more than ~15 lines of supporting definitions,
(b) it duplicates an already collected goal up to renaming, (c) the hit is not actually an
integrability goal (hypothesis-only occurrence, iff used as a rewrite, a different notion such as
`VectorMeasure.Integrable`).

Judgement calls in applying these reasons:
* (a) was used when the goal involves project objects whose only usable property is a project lemma
  that is, up to algebra, the goal itself (abstracting would make the entry a tautology), or when
  inlining needs more than ~15 lines. Where the proof used weaker, separate facts (per-summand
  integrability, continuity, a uniform bound, ...) the object was abstracted instead and kept.
  Existential "construct a Schwartz kernel such that ... ∧ Integrable ..." statements in quasi-rh were
  counted under (a).
* (c) includes PhysLean's `VectorMeasure.Integrable` hits (4 skips). In Mathlib this is an `abbrev`
  for `MeasureTheory.Integrable f μ.variation`, so these are arguably integrability goals for the
  total-variation measure of a complex spectral measure; they were skipped as a different predicate.
* (b) was applied to goals equal up to renaming or instantiation of parameters in the same local
  context; goals with the same integrand but different hypotheses in context were kept separately.

| project               | entries | of which domination | records drawn | skips (a / b / c) |
|-----------------------|---------|---------------------|---------------|-------------------|
| PrimeNumberTheoremAnd | 45      | 4                   | 50            | 1 / 3 / 1         |
| quasi-rh              | 20      | 2                   | 33            | 13 / 0 / 0        |
| PhysLean              | 15      | 2                   | 21            | 1 / 0 / 5         |
| TauCeti               | 15      | 2                   | 17            | 1 / 0 / 1         |
| carleson + brownian   | 5       | 0                   | 9             | 2 / 2 / 0         |
| total                 | 100     | 10                  | 130           | 18 / 5 / 7        |

By kind: 57 `have`, 33 `statement`, 10 `domination`.
By domain: 25 whole line, 21 bounded interval, 15 interval integral, 11 half-line,
16 ℝⁿ/product, 12 abstract measure.
By integrand: 31 explicit, 32 explicit with opaque factor, 37 abstract.

## Conventions

* Variables and hypotheses the goal depends on are binders. For a `have`, the binders are the
  relevant part of its local context (enclosing hypotheses and preceding `have`s it relies on).
* A project definition is inlined when it unfolds in a few lines to Mathlib terms; otherwise it is a
  variable function together with exactly the hypotheses the original proof used about it. Each
  entry's `notes:` says what was inlined or abstracted.
* `domination` entries state the integrability-plus-a.e.-bound hypotheses of the dominated
  convergence lemma that the project called, with the project's *concrete* bound, as a conjunction
  `Integrable bound μ ∧ ∀ᵐ …` (in the shape that lemma expects: `∀ᶠ n, ∀ᵐ a, …`, `∀ n, ∀ᵐ a, …`,
  or `∀ᵐ a, ∀ x ∈ s, …`).
* `original proof:` records how the project proved the goal, in one phrase.
-/


